WEB SOURCE (THE spec): web/src/components/charts/ChartTimeRangeContext.tsx
                       (+ its store web/src/components/charts/cursorSync.ts)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The web source is NOT a visual surface. It is a headless chart cursor-sync
coordination primitive:
  - cursorSync.ts: a module-level external store keyed by syncId, value =
    string | number | null, pub/sub listeners, set/get/clear,
    useCursorSyncPosition, _resetCursorSyncStore. No-op when value unchanged;
    null clears the entry.
  - ChartTimeRangeContext.tsx: React context { syncId, syncMethod } + hooks
    useChartSync (context or null outside provider), useSyncedCursor (props
    { syncId, syncMethod, onMouseMove } or {} outside provider),
    useSyncedReferenceLineX (persistent X value or null). The provider clears
    its syncId's cursor on unmount.

Because cursor sync is a SYNCHRONOUS IN-PROCESS primitive (no network read),
the web source has NO loading / error / stale / offline states. Its observable
states (all reproduced natively) are:
  - empty    : no chart hovered -> SyncedReferenceLineX == None (no line)
  - active   : a cursor value is set -> non-none X every synced chart renders
  - outside-provider fallbacks : useChartSync null / useSyncedCursor {} /
    useSyncedReferenceLineX null  (ChartSync.Use* with a null provider)
This honest carve-out matches the sibling VisuallyHidden surface, which is the
same kind of synchronous in-process channel. i18n keys extracted from source:
NONE (the surface is anonymous; renders no titles/labels) -> no P1/S10 keys to
resolve. Interactive elements: NONE (it is a transparent provider) -> a11y is
the view's transparency (AccessibilityView.Raw) + PII-safe diagnostics.

